data.content:
Here's the full migration. Your code has **4 breaking patterns** in .NET 8:

| API | Problem in .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete, unreliable (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9) |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be woken by a token |
| `while (true)` | No cooperative exit mechanism |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Expected on shutdown — don't propagate.
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline
        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

## What changed and why

| Old | New | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<string>` | Channel is a thread-safe async producer-consumer queue — replaces all three |
| `Thread` + `while(true)` | `Task.Run` + `await foreach` | `ReadAllAsync(token)` blocks cooperatively and responds to cancellation |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Signals the reader to drain remaining items and exit cleanly |
| `Thread.Abort()` (5s fallback) | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter(5s)` | Hard deadline — `ReadAllAsync` throws `OperationCanceledException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | The cooperative equivalent of the abort exception |

---

## Key pitfalls to watch for

1. **`CancellationToken` doesn't interrupt `Handle(item)`** — if `Handle` does long-running work, pass `token` into it and check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at safe points. Otherwise cancellation waits until the current item finishes.

2. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — it leaks timer handles if you use `CancelAfter`. Always `Dispose()` it (the class now implements `IDisposable`).

3. **Graceful vs. hard stop** — `Writer.Complete()` lets the loop drain remaining items. `CancelAfter(5s)` is the hard cutoff. If you want immediate stop with no drain, call `_cts.Cancel()` directly instead of completing the writer.

4. **Race on multiple items** — the original code had a bug: if two `Enqueue` calls happened before `WaitOne` returned, the event was set twice but `Reset()` + single `Dequeue()` only processed one item. `Channel` fixes this — `ReadAllAsync` naturally drains all available items.

5. **If you must keep synchronous `Stop()`**:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop()
   {
       _channel.Writer.Complete();
       _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
       _runTask?.GetAwaiter().GetResult(); // blocks synchronously
   }
   ```
   Prefer `StopAsync` to avoid deadlock risk in UI/ASP.NET contexts.
